= Shapes of Molecules 3

== Molecular Polarity

Polarity arrow always points from the less electronegative atom to the more electronegative atom. The electron is not shared equally between two atoms.

We can use molecular shapes and electronegativity to predict the direction of bonds and molecular polarity.

The molecule doesn't necessarily need to be polar even if it has polar bonds. If the dipole points in symmetric directions and cancel out, the molecule can be nonpolar.

== Cis and Trans Isomers

- The *cis isomer is polar*, and the bond dipole add up.
  - Molecular dipole is sum of bond dipoles.
- The *trans isomer is nonpolar*, and the bond dipoles cancel out.
  - The molecule is symmetric.
